    "created_at": "2022-12-02T21:01:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-12-02T21:01:11Z",
    "body": "To expand on Matan's point: this change would makes debugging a common issue harder for beginning users, with the advantage of saving some typing for a complex setup.\r\n\r\nWe get quite a few users who install starship but don't correctly add it to `PATH`, resulting in some sort of \"starship executable not found\" message (in fact, I'm pretty sure we got one less than 24 hours ago on Discord, although I'm not certain because my Spanish is lousy).\r\n\r\nThe proposed change makes it so that instead of getting an error message, these users will simply not see starship. Now, instead of reading and understanding the error message, or using that error message to ask for help, they have to do an additional song and dance:\r\n  - Did I really follow the instructions correctly? Maybe I have to run it again?\r\n  - Maybe I have to restart my terminal emulator?\r\n  - Maybe I have to restart my computer?\r\n\r\nOnce none of these work, they then have to come to us with a \"I did the instructions and nothing changed\" bug, which are always incredibly nebulous since there's basically no additional information. Even if we're aware that the starship init command is a potential failure point, the first thing we then have to tell them to do is \"run this alternate command mode, see if you get an error, and come back to us\", which adds an additional round-trip of debugging.\r\n\r\nIf the benefits here outweighed the costs, I might be amenable to it, but as far as I can tell, the upside here is \"users who want to deploy across multi-config systems can save a few minutes.\" But the absence of binaries is likely going to be a problem for many different programs (e.g. in my fish and zsh configs, I have a bunch of functions for how to deal with the neovim/vim dichotomy), so this doesn't even save you *that* much time, because now you need to write some shell code for those other binaries as well.\r\n\r\nI'm sympathetic to the multi-system use case---my `yadm` configs are currently filled with nonsense about how to do initialization correctly across 4 different platforms---but I think introducing this as a default is going to create more issues than it solves.",
